 Reflection </think> <answer> There is a series pattern that can be observed from the condition given in the question.&nbsp;
1. If Petunia has the mail on a day, then one house either side will not have mail on that day. &nbsp;
2. There cannot be any three consecutive houses without mail on the same day. &nbsp;
only three primary different combinations are possible for the three consecutive houses as viewd from Petunia's house.  &nbsp;
Therefore if the house with mail is either towards petunia 1,2 or a. not at all then two different arrangements can be made.  &nbsp;
Another interesting note is that out of these three houses the mail is delivered in three ways. ( mail ,no mail,<strong>exactly one house without mail </strong>,and <strong>all houses without mail  </strong>)  Now if Petunia house is not getting mail then we have two more combinations that is <strong>no mail and exactly one house with mail </strong>and <strong>all houses with mail </strong>.  &nbsp; 
Considering this we can develop a formula for the required combinations.  &nbsp;<img src="http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?C<sub>2</sub>*3<sub>x</sub">&nbsp;" title="C<sub>2</sub>*3<sub>x</sub>"/>
where

$$C<sub>2</sub>$$ is a coefficent notated here (combination) which can take the values 0,1,2 and
$$3<sub>x</sub>$$ is a variable notated here x which can take the values 0,1,2,3,4 and 5
the basic relation $3<sub>x</sub>$ is derived from the fact that for 1st house={ mail ,no mail,<strong>exactly one house without mail </strong>},for 2nd house={ mail, no mail, exactly one house without mail} Taken together these 2 gives us the first 4 choices for 2 houses out of which 1 house can not get mail.  Therefore $3<sub>x</sub>$ also gives the value 1 or 3 for the following conditions 1) one house without mail and 2) all houses without mail.  &nbsp;<img src="http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?3<sub>3</sub>" title="3<sub>3</sub>"/>
